(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_ecosystem=[{id:"neet-2026-q094",pn:94,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ecosystem productivity",q:`Match List I with List II :
A. Productivity \u2014 I. Gross primary productivity minus respiration losses
B. Net primary productivity \u2014 II. Rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers
C. Gross primary productivity \u2014 III. Rate of biomass production
D. Secondary productivity \u2014 IV. Rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],c:1,s:`Productivity is the rate of biomass production.
Net primary productivity is gross primary productivity (GPP) minus respiration losses (R).
Gross primary productivity is the rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis.
Secondary productivity is the rate of formation of new organic matter by consumers.
So A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.`},{id:"neet-2026-q099",pn:99,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Decomposition process terms",q:`Match List I with List II:
List-I \u2014 List-II
A. Decomposition \u2014 I. Accumulation of dark coloured amorphous colloidal substance
B. Detritus \u2014 II. Release of inorganic nutrients by the activity of microbes in soil
C. Mineralisation \u2014 III. Breaking down of complex organic matter into inorganic substances.
D. Humification \u2014 IV. Dead remains of plants and animals including fecal matter`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],c:2,s:`Decomposition is breaking down of complex organic matter into inorganic substances.
Detritus is the dead remains of plants and animals including fecal matter; it is the raw material for decomposition.
Degradation of humus by microbes that releases inorganic nutrients is called mineralisation.
Accumulation of the dark coloured amorphous substance called humus is humification.`},{id:"neet-2026-q149",pn:149,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ecological pyramids",q:"Ecological pyramids represent the relationship between the organisms at different trophic levels and they are generally inverted for:",o:["Pyramid of energy in pond ecosystem","Pyramid of biomass in sea","Pyramid of number in grassland","Pyramid of biomass in grassland"],c:1,s:`Pyramid of number in grassland ecosystem is upright.
Pyramid of energy in pond ecosystem is upright.
Pyramid of biomass in grassland is upright.
Pyramid of biomass in sea is inverted.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q161",pn:161,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Food chain, trophic levels",q:"Which of the following are primary consumers in a food chain?",o:["Predators","Herbivores","Carnivores","Parasites"],c:1,s:"Primary consumers in a food chain are the herbivores."},{id:"neet-2026r-q178",pn:178,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Energy flow",q:`Which of the following statements are Correct?
(a) Energy flow from producers to consumers is unidirectional
(b) Energy pyramid can never be inverted
(c) Transfer of energy follows the 1% law
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(a) and (b) only","(a) and (c) only","(b) and (c) only","(a), (b) and (c)"],c:0,s:`Energy flow is unidirectional: plants capture solar energy, then food passes from producers to decomposers.
The pyramid of energy is always upright and can never be inverted, because some energy is lost as heat at each step.
Energy transfer follows the 10 per cent law: only 10 per cent of the energy passes to the next trophic level.
So (c), the 1% law, is wrong.`},{id:"neet-2025-q100",pn:100,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Productivity and energy flow",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I : The primary source of energy in an ecosystem is solar energy.
Statement II : The rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis in an ecosystem is called net primary productivity (NPP).
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both statement I and statement II are correct","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct"],c:2,s:`The primary source of energy in an ecosystem is solar energy.
The rate of production of organic matter during photosynthesis is gross primary productivity, not net primary productivity.
Hence statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2025-q150",pn:150,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ecosystem productivity",q:"Which of the following is the unit of productivity of an Ecosystem?",o:["gm\u207B\xB2","KCal m\u207B\xB2","KCal m\u207B\xB3","(KCal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9"],c:3,s:`The rate of biomass production is called productivity.
It is expressed in gm\u207B\xB2yr\u207B\xB9 or (KCal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9 to compare the productivity of different ecosystems.`},{id:"neet-2025-q170",pn:170,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Energy flow",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: In ecosystem, there is unidirectional flow of energy of sun from producers to consumers.
Statement II: Ecosystems are exempted from 2nd law of thermodynamics.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both statement I and statement II are correct","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct"],c:2,s:`The sun is the only source of energy for all ecosystems on Earth, except the deep sea hydro-thermal ecosystem.
Energy flow is unidirectional from the sun to producers and then to consumers.
Ecosystems are not exempted from the second law of thermodynamics.
They need a constant supply of energy to make the molecules they need, to counter the universal tendency towards increasing disorderliness.`},{id:"neet-2024-q146",pn:146,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Productivity",q:`In an ecosystem if the Net Primary Productivity (NPP) of first trophic level is:
100x (kcal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9 what would be the GPP (Gross Primary Productivity) of the third trophic level of the same ecosystem?`,o:["10 x (kcal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9","(100x)/(3x) (kcal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9","(x)/(10) (kcal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9","x(kcal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9"],c:3,s:`The source gives no solution for this question.
Using the 10% law: the second trophic level gets about 10x and the third about x (kcal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9.
So the printed answer is x (kcal m\u207B\xB2)yr\u207B\xB9.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here. The printed answer is [4].`},{id:"neet-2023-q104",pn:104,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Decomposition and detritus food chain",q:`Identify the correct statements :
A. Detrivores perform fragmentation
B. The humus if further degraded by some microbes during mineralization
C. Water soluble inorganic nutrients go down into the soil and get precipitated by a process called leaching
D. The detritus food chain begins with living organisms
E. Earthworms break down detritus into smaller particles by a process called catabolism.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["D, E, A only","A, B, C only","B, C, D only","C, D, E only"],c:1,s:`The detritus food chain begins with detritus, which is dead organic matter, not living organisms. So D is wrong.
Breaking detritus into smaller particles is fragmentation, done by earthworms. Catabolism is done by saprotrophic bacteria and fungi, which turn detritus into simple inorganic substances. So E is wrong.
A, B and C are correct.`},{id:"neet-2023-q107",pn:107,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Primary productivity",q:`In the equation
GPP \u2013 R = NPP
GPP is Gross Primary Productivity
NPP is Net Primary Productivity
R here is ____________.`,o:["Reproductive allocation","Photosynthetically active radiation","Respiratory quotient","Respiratory loss"],c:3,s:`Plants use a large part of GPP in respiration.
GPP minus the respiratory losses (R) gives NPP.
So R is the respiratory loss.`},{id:"neet-2023-q133",pn:133,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ozone depletion units",q:"The thickness of ozone in a column of air in the atmosphere is measured in terms of :",o:["Kilobase","Dobson units","Decibels","Decameter"],c:1,s:`The thickness of ozone in a column of air from ground to top of the atmosphere is measured in Dobson units (DU).
Noise is measured in decibels.
Note: Environmental issues has no chapter in the list; Ecosystem used as nearest.`},{id:"neet-2023-q139",pn:139,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Water pollution and eutrophication",q:"Which one of the following statements is NOT correct?",o:["The amount of some toxic substances of industrial waste water increases in the organisms at successive trophic levels","The micro-organisms involved in biodegradation of organic matter in a sewage polluted water body consume a lot of oxygen causing the death of aquatic organisms","Algal blooms caused by excess of organic matter in water improve water quality and promote fisheries","Water hyacinth grows abundantly in eutrophic water bodies and leads to an imbalance in the ecosystem dynamics of the water body"],c:2,s:`Algal blooms give the water a colour, spoil water quality and cause fish deaths.
So the statement that they improve quality and promote fisheries is wrong.
Note: Environmental issues has no chapter in the list; Ecosystem used as nearest.`},{id:"neet-2023-q167",pn:167,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Water pollution and biomagnification",q:"Which of the following statements is correct?",o:["Algal Bloom decreases fish mortality","Eutrophication refers to increase in domestic sewage and waste water in lakes.","Biomagnification refers to increase in concentration of the toxicant at successive trophic levels.","Presence of large amount of nutrients in water restricts 'Algal Bloom'."],c:2,s:`Biomagnification is the rise in concentration of a toxicant at each successive trophic level.
Lots of nutrients in water promote algal bloom, and algal bloom increases fish mortality.
Eutrophication is the natural ageing of a lake by nutrient enrichment.`},{id:"neet-2023-q173",pn:173,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Air pollution control",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Electrostatic precipitator is most widely used in thermal power plant
Statement II : Electrostatic precipitator in thermal power plant removes ionising radiations
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."],c:3,s:`Electrostatic precipitators are the most widely used device in thermal power plants.
They remove over 99 percent of the particulate matter in the exhaust, not ionising radiation.
So Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2022-q122",pn:122,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Air pollution control devices",q:"The device which can remove particulate matter present in the exhaust from a thermal power plant is :",o:["Incinerator","Electrostatic Precipitator","Catalytic Convertor","STP"],c:1,s:`An electrostatic precipitator removes over 99% of particulate matter from power plant exhaust.
Catalytic converters are for vehicles.
STPs treat sewage.`},{id:"neet-2022-q130",pn:130,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Decomposition",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Decomposition is a process in which the detritus is degraded into simpler substances by microbes.
Statement II: Decomposition is faster if the detritus is rich in lignin and chitin.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:1,s:`Decomposers break complex organic matter into simple inorganic substances, so Statement I is correct.
The rate depends on the chemical make-up of the detritus and on climate.
Detritus rich in lignin and chitin decomposes slowly.
Detritus rich in nitrogen and water-soluble sugars decomposes faster.
So Statement II is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2022-q148",pn:148,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Phosphorus cycle",q:"Which one of the following will accelerate phosphorus cycle?",o:["Volcanic activity","Weathering of rocks","Rain fall and storms","Burning of fossil fuels"],c:1,s:`The phosphorus cycle is a sedimentary cycle.
Its reservoir is the earth's crust (lithosphere).
Weathering of rocks releases phosphorus and speeds up the cycle.`},{id:"neet-2022-q181",pn:181,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Decomposition",q:"Detritivores breakdown detritus into smaller particles. This process is called:",o:["Fragmentation","Humification","Decomposition","Catabolism"],c:0,s:`Detritivores such as earthworms break detritus into smaller pieces.
This step is called fragmentation.`}]})();
